+{"id":"bigger","len":25,"text":"Make it a little bigger. "}
+{"id":"unsupported-link","len":78,"text":"I just got a notification from the Claude Code app that says unsupported link."}
+{"id":"whisper-segments","len":146,"text":"So explain why Whisper has this segments thing. If it, it seems like it's sort of a streaming interface, what's the purpose of the 15 second cap? "}
+{"id":"decisions-tab-screenshot","len":255,"text":"Take a screenshot of that Open Router Brains Decisions tab. The tabs don't seem to work and the Decisions tab doesn't have any content. You know, so part of the page loads, but uh, a screenshot should reveal that whatever is supposed to be in there isn't."}
+{"id":"cards-and-logs-subdomain","len":394,"text":"I'm seeing in my, my console log, uh, a directory code slash loo 42 slash worktrees slash cards and logs. And then cards and logs has its own URL, cards and logs.localhost. Um, so I, I guess that's just a worktree, which is okay. Is the, uh, cards and logs like subdomain, like kind of, does that get pollute the namespace or are these all temporary and they get, they could be recycled easily?"}
+{"id":"token-budget-bursts","len":543,"text":"Well, it's definitely work on staying under budget on tokens. Like, um, I mean, I, I think if, if there's a use for burning tokens, like if we have a five hour window that's about to, to collapse and we have plenty of uh, if we're way under pace, and we're, there's not much else in you know that we're planning for, then burning a bunch of tokens can be useful. And so you can just spawn a whole bunch of agents to kind of like test out whether uh, you can get better results or unique outcomes or whatever by by having a consensus of agents."}
+{"id":"capability-before-cost","len":654,"text":"So of all the different uh, models, though, well, there's so there's DeepSeek, there's Quen, there's Gemma, Google's open source models. There's all kinds of different models, and I mean, honestly, leaning on some of the open source ones, they should be way cheaper. But if they're way less capable, you know, what I've learned is that the capability of a model is almost more important than the cost. So, like. You know, it's it's better to just pay a little more and get it done properly than pay a tenth the cost and not get, you know, get a tenth the result means that like you're not getting much of anything and it may or may not be correct at all."}
+{"id":"page-api-review-fix","len":773,"text":"Second review (review.md) says fix. There are three small changes, all in qf-1, same fence:\n(1) Finding 1, which is the owner's own ask (\"this is a Page, and its property X = Y\"). On the core/Page API tab, draw the card from a small live example, such as `new Page({ title: \"Intro\", children: \"setup usage\" })` or whatever minimal instance constructs cleanly without mounting. Values must show: title = \"Intro\", and so on. Keep the chips linking to api/<name>/.\n(2) Finding 3: the readme names both `doc` and `api`, and says `api` wins.\n(3) Finding 5: have own_methods() walk up to (but not including) Object.prototype, or retitle the instance demo. Choose whichever is simpler.\nRe-shoot object/api-1920.png via the tab, run the smoke test, commit, and reply with one line."}
+{"id":"is-the-test-a-good-test","len":1525,"text":"But yeah, I, I guess I was just thinking about the, the way that the AI tests are stored and rendered and, you know, how many different models they've been applied to. And really the, the weighting of the test, it's, it's sort of like, a, is the test a good test? Um, if you can get a fairly like, okay, so it's sort of like the variance in the completion rate. If the completion rate is, you know, close to one, then it might not be the best test. You're only going to weed out the a small minority of, of the worst models that fail the test. If the completion rate is near zero, then it's just a bad test. It's like nobody's figuring it out. This is somebody messed up the architecture of this test. If the completion rate is anywhere in that kind of... not zero and not a hundred percent range then maybe there's some legitimacy to the test and i would say i i wouldn't i wouldn't necessarily say a 50 percent completion rate is like the optimal test but it is sort of like a very indicative like okay there's something going on here if 50 percent of models pass this test and 50 percent of models fail this test still it could be an indicator of a bad test maybe the models that are failing aren't actually failing that badly The criteria is just a little bit, you know, strict. And so they did something that was slightly incomplete, mostly correct, but being judged as incorrect based on the, the scoring criteria. So we don't, we want to avoid that. And I'm not sure how to do that, but you'll figure it out. Um, yeah."}
+{"id":"research-process-fast-assistants","len":2601,"text":"All right, so I believe we did a research task about using uh, using the um, open router stuff, right? So what I want you to do is spawn a minion, and it it could be it could use the same work tree if it still exists. Um, but what I want you to do is to look at that research process. From like a fresh angle and think you know what was asked what was done and how did the research process actually work and so i don't know if the Cervex mastermind should be a part of this um in terms of updating the actual skill system like the Cervex mastermind seems to be the one who's got the context to uh get that done properly um but you know i, I was asking about getting the research process kind of baked into this AI process for any Uh, for any card, for any page, for whatever context, you know, we're, we're going to, uh, well, and okay, another thing I was just thinking that the Cervex Mastermind should think about is do we need and should we be creating fast assistance for every new kind of context, session context, or however we're describing this? Um, you know, the fast assistant needs to be fast and so can't get bogged down in, you know, other tasks or whatever. And so you, You kind of want a fast assistant for every context. However, um, we don't need to keep them running. So I'm not sure how that works in the SDK. I've seen these idle agents, you know, on lots of screens. It uh, seems like maybe that's just, um, maybe, maybe there's a small piece of in-memory like structure in the JavaScript SDK that, you know, can tap into that idle agent and reuse it, but at pretty low cost. We might need to purge those at some point if we're, you know, working across a lot of different pages and start spawning hundreds of fast assistants. I guess the alternative is to recycle them or, well, but the thing is, if you, if you kind of, if you do, well, you don't want, you don't want to have context bloat. So you kind of want to start fresh. And so it seems like starting a new, you know, fast assistant for a new kind of context mean is exactly what, you know, well, whether we pre-make them or make them, I think making them on demand. So only when, you know, like the dictate button is pushed, then if there isn't currently a fast assistant, we make one. Um, I don't think we need to, every time a new page is made, we automatically uh, create, you know, a bunch of agents that just sit around and potentially never do anything. So, you know, add that to, well, send that, that, you know, issue through to the uh, system designer so we can at least make note and try and optimize for that."}
